<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Vakataka dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">250 CE – 500 CE (approx.) · capital Nandivardhana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading power of the Deccan in the Gupta age, allied to the Guptas by marriage and patrons of the painted caves of Ajanta.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The dynasty was founded in the mid-3rd century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vindhyashakti">Vindhyashakti</a> in the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidarbha">Vidarbha</a>, as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty</a> broke up. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravarasena-i">Pravarasena I</a>, the only Vakataka to call himself emperor, performed four horse sacrifices and extended rule across central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. After him the realm split into a main branch at Nandivardhana and a western branch at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatsagulma">Vatsagulma</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Vakatakas were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> who patronised Vedic ritual and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaiva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnava</a> cults. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudrasena-ii">Rudrasena II</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prabhavatigupta">Prabhavatigupta</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-ii">Chandragupta II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>, who ruled as regent after his death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravarasena-ii">Pravarasena II</a> wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setubandha">Setubandha</a> and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravarapura">Pravarapura</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harishena-vakataka">Harishena</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatsagulma">Vatsagulma</a> branch, his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varahadeva">Varahadeva</a> and other nobles sponsored many of the finest caves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajanta-caves">Ajanta</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harishena-vakataka">Harishena</a> the Vakatakas vanish suddenly in the late 5th century, for reasons that are unclear. Their lands passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vishnukundina-dynasty">Vishnukundina dynasty</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kalachuris-of-mahishmati">Kalachuris of Mahishmati</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/vakataka-dynasty.jpg" alt="Vakataka dynasty" width="320" height="484"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ajanta_Padmapani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>250 CE – 500 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nandivardhana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pravarasena I, Pravarasena II, Harishena</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit, Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vakatakas, Vakataka Empire</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=375&amp;focus=vakataka-dynasty" aria-label="Vakataka dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6740 1312 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Vakataka dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7060 1568L7165 1820M7060 1568L7362 1550M7060 1568L7156 1800"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7165 1820h0M7362